WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.984
Today is Tuesday, June 2nd, 1992, and this is a session of Al Farouq, and the subject is Christopher Columbus. Of course, always I had my ideas and so on. But one of the things I did that nobody had done, and not because they don't, they can't, but because they don't, they don't want to, is I examined his diaries. He wrote diaries, you know.

00:00:29.984 --> 00:00:41.882
And in his diaries, you read things which is really baffles and bothers the mind. And and of course, I Spanish is my first language. I know it very well.

00:00:41.882 --> 00:01:03.221
P. A. T. from the University of Barcelona, which is not a small thing, you know, et cetera. But I would have liked some of you to read it because if I read, people are thinking, "Oh, I think cannot be this," you know. But so we don't have. How fluent are you in Spanish? Perfect. That's it. We're going to see. All right.

00:01:03.221 --> 00:01:11.311
We're going to see tonight. We're going to test you, of spies. All right. Um.

00:01:11.311 --> 00:01:41.171
So Mickey, you have to give. Listen, you have to give the order. Sure. He said, "Wait a few seconds, Rabbi. Tell us about who you're going to do and why." I'm going to. No, I wanted this when the people come.

00:01:41.171 --> 00:02:08.354
All right. We're going to begin right right now. Of course, this lecture, our community, and I think Judaism, had lost. We had we suffered a great loss this Friday night when.

00:02:08.354 --> 00:02:22.787
Dennis Zweig passed away, and I tell you why is a great loss because he was the first Sephardic rabbi.

00:02:22.787 --> 00:02:44.269
To have been produced in this community exclusively by the mechanism of the community. We do have other rabbis, but other rabbis were educated, and that's not in a way has a lot to to to to minimize their accomplishments, but they were educated in Ashkenazi yeshivot.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:55.985
He was the product of the Sephardic tradition, and he behaved as such. He was a businessman. He never wore a kippa in the street.

00:02:55.985 --> 00:03:19.102
He was dealing with with people who were living some some of them at the fringe of Judaism, and he always behaved according to both the letter and the spirit of the Torah.

00:03:19.101 --> 00:03:29.683
It is easy for somebody who grows a beard and has a black head and has this and this—I don't know—to behave according.

00:03:29.683 --> 00:03:53.265
To the persecutions of the law, but if you are a young man dressed like everybody else, with any none of the paraphernalia, and you observe the Torah in a way that brings honor and respect, that is something a very special accomplishment. And I tell you why I call him a rabbi.

00:03:53.265 --> 00:04:17.593
Because precisely, because he did not have the title of a rabbi, he would have been offended even if you call him a rabbi. That's why he was a rabbi. The true rabbi is that individual who does not feel worthy of the title, who does not want you to apply this title to him. I once I told him, Dennis.

00:04:17.593 --> 00:04:34.836
I want you to spend a few months in Israel and get semikhah rabbanut. I'll get you semikhah rabbanut. Yes, I have a friend of mine who's a rabbi, rabbi Rashi in Pristiv. And he says, I don't want to. I don't need it.

00:04:34.836 --> 00:04:58.808
And that is something special. So, I am dedicating this lecture to his memory, and I hope that we could dedicate his memory to our lives. We'll be richer, we'll be better people. So, now we to the lecture. The subject of the lecture is nothing to do with Torah. Throughout this year, we accomplished, I think, greatly.

00:04:58.808 --> 00:05:16.252
We studied the entire book of Daniel in a very profound, very intelligent manner, and we studied many Psalms and of Tehillim. But today I want to speak not about the text of the Torah.

00:05:16.252 --> 00:05:29.780
But about Jewish history, and I want to speak about an individual that single-handedly changed the world.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:42.417
He physically changed the world, and that is Columbus. Columbus is the person who, in 1492.

00:05:42.417 --> 00:06:01.777
Through a vision that he had, and we'll speak about it, he, in a sense, ushered modernity. Whatever the world today is, it is stained to the vision of one single individual, Columbus.

00:06:01.777 --> 00:06:29.215
We are going to speak about his identity, who this person was, what his ideas were, and I want to offer some data which deliberately is omitted by historians. And this will show you also that Jewish history is yet to be written. All right? Now.

00:06:29.215 --> 00:06:53.192
The personality of Columbus is clouded in mystery, and there are two reasons for it. Reason number one, as we're going to see it shortly, historians deliberately omit certain passages, refuse to look into certain material.

00:06:53.192 --> 00:07:01.604
And therefore, we don't know much about the life of Columbus. Second.

00:07:01.604 --> 00:07:21.686
Columbus himself, when he speaks about himself, he uses very cryptic phrases. He's never open. He does not tell you all the things. And because of these two reasons.

00:07:21.686 --> 00:07:33.265
Very little is known about the lavas. Let me begin by charting one map. First of all.

00:07:33.265 --> 00:07:42.410
The name Columbus. We usually we say, of course, that he is Italian.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:08:09.278
He was born in Genoa. This is true. The family of Columbus, they were weavers. And I want to begin first with the name Columbus. Of course, he was never called Columbus. Columbus is the Latin way. He was called two two names. He had Colombo and Colón.

00:08:09.278 --> 00:08:24.252
In Spanish, he was called Colón, and when he was in in Italy, he was called Colombo. Now, just let me tell you that Judeo-Spanish, Spanish spoken by the Jews.

00:08:24.252 --> 00:08:33.226
Colombo is the translation of a very simple name, Yona.

00:08:33.226 --> 00:09:02.871
Yes, Jonah, a pigeon, is translated in Judeo-Spanish. Colomba, Colomba for the for the female, and Colombo for the for the male. So Colombo, it comes to translate the word Jonah, and it is a very popular Sephardic name. The last rabbi of Livorno, before the Second World War, he was Alavashalom Rabbi Yosef Colombo.

00:09:02.871 --> 00:09:21.794
You look into the into the Jewish encyclopedia under Colombo, and you find many many chachamim. The other name, Kolon. Those who are acquainted with Jewish law of Chizalacha know that one of the greatest poskim we all had was Maharik.

00:09:21.794 --> 00:09:34.990
A Mahadik is was Rabbi Yosef, Kolon, yes. So both names, are were popular, in um in Jewish history, right.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:43.465
Now I want to shatter one very important myth, and that is going to create a question.

00:09:43.465 --> 00:10:04.184
